Trump acknowledges Iran played role in Republican midterm losses






                US President Donald Trump acknowledged that the war on Iran played a role in Republicans' losses in the midterm elections.

"They'll send over things that are insane, and I'll veto them," Trump said in an interview with Fox News. "And after a couple of months of vetoes, they'll call and they'll say, 'How do we get this stuff done?' And we'll work together."

"I think a part of it was Iran, but at the same time people understood the importance of what I've done with Iran," he added.

In another interview with The New York Times, the American president said he was willing to accept the results of the midterm elections.&nbsp;

"We'll work with the Democrats, and we’ll, you know, look forward to working with them. We’ll probably work something out," Trump said.&nbsp;

Aipac and the Michigan Senate race: How the Israel lobby became toxic






                "A lobby is like a night flower: it thrives in the dark and dies in the sun.” So&nbsp;said Steve Rosen, former foreign policy director of the&nbsp;American Israel Public Affairs Committee (Aipac)&nbsp;in a memo to a fellow staffer. Rosen would later be fired amid&nbsp;a major scandal&nbsp;involving espionage in passing classified&nbsp;US&nbsp;information to&nbsp;Israel.&nbsp;

Rosen was correct about the danger to a lobbying organisation that publicity poses. But in December 2021, Aipac decided to test that theory by creating two political action committees (Pacs) that enabled it to take part directly in political campaigns.

The results were mixed, and&nbsp;Aipac recently announced&nbsp;that it was closing one of the Pacs it had set up.&nbsp;

Aipac had been an advocacy organisation, promoting Israel’s interests, but it did not fund campaigns directly, nor did it endorse or fund political candidates.&nbsp;

In many ways, that made it more effective. It could do things like issue a congressional report card which rated every member of Congress’s support of Israel. Then other groups that were actual Pacs could use those reports to decide which candidates they should be contributing to.&nbsp;

Read more:&nbsp;Aipac and the Michigan Senate race: How the Israel lobby became toxic

Morning update 






                Good morning Middle East Eye readers,&nbsp;

Here are the latest developments:&nbsp;



	Reports confirmed that the US military was responsible for overnight strikes in southern Iran's Qesh Island and Bandar Lengeh. The strikes come despite US President Donald Trump's remarks saying he would not launch attacks against Iran after the midterms.&nbsp;


	US President Donald Trump said that Washington may need to "go after" a suspected underground nuclear complex in Iran, known as Pickaxe Mountain, if there is no deal reached. "We might have to go after Pickaxe, we can do that in one hour, as soon as we want, at any time we want," Trump told Fox News. Trump also did not rule out the possibility of resuming the war on Iran.


	An Iranian military spokesperson has warned that any renewed attacks by the US will be met with a "harder and broader" response compared to Tehran's past retaliations. He said that if a new war broke out, then the "geography of the conflict will not remain limited to the West Asia region", adding that US "facilities, equipment, personnel, and interests in any location within reach could become targets"


	Iran's parliament speaker and chief negotiator in talks with the US said that the ball was in Washington's court to "prevent war". "The countries of the region should know that, just as the Islamic Republic of Iran stands ready for battle with its finger on the trigger, it has also given the American side a powerful and rational response," Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf said in a televised speech.


	Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi indicated that talks between Tehran and Saudi Arabia are ongoing. "In my interview with Al Jazeera network, I affirmed that we harbour no hostility toward the countries of the Gulf Cooperation Council, and we appreciate the positive role played by Qatar and Oman, and we continue the dialogue with Saudi Arabia," he said in a post on X.


	The Yemeni army said that it launched 951 operations targeting Houthi positions over the past 24 hours as part of Operation Dawn of Yemen. Army spokesperson Majed al-Nuzaili claimed in a post on X that the operations had "neutralized" around 332 Houthi field commanders and fighters.


	One Indian was killed after an attack on a commercial vessel near Limah in Oman, the Indian foreign ministry said on Tuesday. Among the 14 Indian nationals on board the MSV AL JUSUFI, 13 were rescued after the incident occurred on Monday.

Report: US launched strikes on Iran






                The US military is responsible for strikes on Iran, according to The Wall Street Journal citing an unnamed US official.&nbsp;

The newspaper indicated that the attacks were part of a series of limited operations Washington had "secretly carried out" against the country.&nbsp;

Earlier, several explosions were heard in southern Iran's&nbsp;&nbsp;Qeshm Island in the Strait of Hormuz as well as in the coastal city of Bandar Lengeh in Hormozgan province.

The strikes come despite US president Donald Trump's remarks saying he would not launch attacks against Iran after the midterms.&nbsp;

Blasts heard in southern Iran 






                Explosions were heard overnight in southern Iran, according to several local reports.&nbsp;

The blasts were reported on&nbsp;Qeshm Island&nbsp;in the Strait of Hormuz&nbsp;as well as ones heard in the coastal city of Bandar Lengeh&nbsp;in Hormozgan province.

US midterm elections 2026: Abdul El-Sayed becomes first Muslim elected to Senate







        Submitted by 
Yasmine El-Sabawi
 on 
Tue, 11/03/2026 - 17:11






                Egyptian-American Democrat condemns use of US tax dollars to support Israeli 'apartheid and genocidal war' in historic victory speech







                  Democratic US Senator-elect Abdul El-Sayed speaks onstage after declaring victory in Michigan's US Senate race during an election night party at The Department at Hudson's in Detroit, Michigan, early on 4 November 2026 (Jeff Kowalsky/AFP)
                  Off
                  Progressive Democrat Abdul El-Sayed won a highly coveted Michigan Senate seat on Wednesday, and will become the first Muslim to serve in the upper chamber of Congress come January.&nbsp;

El-Sayed declared victory with 80 percent of votes counted, showing him in a comfortable 51.8 percent lead compared to his Republican opponent Mike Rogers at 46.2 percent.

The Associated Press declared El-Sayed the winner at&nbsp;2:27 am EST (7:27 GMT)

"I am so proud and honored to stand here as your senator-elect," Sayed said in an energized victory speech, where he said he was able to overcome Islamophobic slurs aimed at him by US President Donald Trump and Vice President JD Vance.

"To all the parents who struggled to explain [to their kids] why the president openly mocked someone for their name or how they pray, let them know tomorrow that the bad guys lost," he said to cheers and applause.&nbsp;









"They want an America that is too small for some of us. We know that America is big enough for all of us," he added.&nbsp;


US Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed celebrated his victory by taking on Aipac and the special interests that poured millions into trying to defeat him https://t.co/Wd2L9m9qdC
— Middle East Eye (@MiddleEastEye) November 4, 2026


Sayed, smiling and responding to lively applause specifically called out Vance and Trump.&nbsp;

"JD, habibi, there is this delicious irony in the fact that you're constitutionally obligated to swear me in in January," he said.&nbsp;

To Trump he said: "Donald, I know you struggle with my name, so after JD swears me in in January, you can just go on and call me Senator".&nbsp;

Sayed's victory marks a watershed moment in US political history.&nbsp;


'We don't want our money sent to do apartheid or genocidal war,'

Abdul El-Sayed


A progressive who shocked the party establishment when he narrowly defeated Haley Stevens, who was backed by pro-Israel lobbying groups, in the August Democratic primary, Sayed was endorsed early by the Senate's leading voice on progressive issues, Bernie Sanders of Vermont.&nbsp;

Sayed specifically challenged the American Israel Public Affairs Committee (Aipac) and other pro-Israel lobbying groups that spent tens of millions of dollars against him.&nbsp;

"To Aipac who spent $42 million against us. Thank you for giving me the opportunity to beat you twice," he said.&nbsp;

"They try to call you hateful if you believe that Palestinian kids should get to grow up in the land of their forefathers without being murdered by our tax dollars," he said. "If you believe in equal rights for peace, diginity and self-determination that's not hate, it's love," he said.&nbsp;

"We want our taxes spent here...we don't want our money sent to do apartheid or genocidal war," he said.&nbsp;

That race proved so pivotal that half a million more Democratic voters showed up to the polls than in previous primary races, which typically see low turnouts,&nbsp;Michigan Democratic Party Chair&nbsp;Curtis Hertel said at the time.&nbsp;

In the later stages of his campaign, Sayed received the backing of former President Barack Obama, who remains one of the most popular figures among the party establishment.&nbsp;

Who is Abdul El-Sayed?

The 42-year-old's full name is Abdulrahman Mohammed El-Sayed. He is married to an Indian-American doctor and has two daughters. When he graduated from the University of Michigan and gave the commencement address in 2007, the event's keynote speaker, former President Bill Clinton, praised his oratory skills at the podium.&nbsp;

The Michigan native then earned a medical degree from Columbia University and led the public health departments in Detroit and Wayne County before launching what was seen as a long-shot bid for the US Senate in 2025.









But it wasn't his first time.

In 2018, Sayed unsuccessfully sought the governorship of Michigan, but he managed to lay the foundations for his vision for Medicare-For-All: the establishment of a national health insurance programme in the US.

As private insurance premiums have skyrocketed in the US since then, that policy proposal grew increasingly popular among those on the left and the right, as did Sayed's criticism of the closeness of the US-Israel relationship - an approach that was very similar to that of the highly popular New York City mayor, Zohran Mamdani.&nbsp;

Sayed has repeatedly said that he has never met with corporate lobbyists, and in both his campaigns, he raised money only from individual donors rather than corporations.&nbsp;

'He's different'

Unlike Mamdani, however, Sayed was not endorsed by the Democratic Socialists of America, as he sought the support of his party's centrists in a volatile swing state like Michigan.&nbsp;

He appeared on as many television networks, podcasts, and social media influencer accounts as he could, in a bid to reach as many segments of the American public as possible, regardless of political affiliation, Sayed often said.&nbsp;

But he was also met with such a disproportionate amount of media scrutiny that he inadvertently became better known - and perhaps more appealing - to voters than his opponent, former Republican Congressman Mike Rogers.









Rogers, who was not under the microscope in the same manner, was endorsed by President Donald Trump at a time when the president's approval ratings were at an all-time low.&nbsp;&nbsp;




  'Despicable betrayal': Haley Stevens endorses Republican candidate over party nominee Abdul El-Sayed
    Read More »


Trump has repeatedly called Sayed&nbsp;"a Communist loser who hates Jews and Israel", and insisted that he would only call him "Mohammed" because that's what the majority of Muslims are called, Trump said.&nbsp;

Republicans also cast doubt on his allegiance to the US Constitution, claiming he had ties to the Muslim Brotherhood because of statements his half-sister once made.

There is no known evidence tying Sayed to the group, which has no central hub and was not designated as a terrorist organisation in the US until Trump sanctioned three of its political branches last year.&nbsp;



Sayed said in August that the focus on his name and identity is "the only play they got" against him.&nbsp;

"It's like, wow, he's different," he said of Republican attacks.

"That's the best you got? You can't actually talk about what you want to do? My name is not raising your gas prices. My name is not taking us to war. My name is not the reason you can't afford your groceries."&nbsp;

