                  Aber Kawas has won her bid for the New York State Senate District 12 seat,&nbsp;taking a 75 percent share of the votes&nbsp;with 90 percent of the votes counted when the race was called by the New York Times.&nbsp;

Kawas has made history as the first hijab-wearing Palestinian American to serve in the New York State Legislature. She joins a small list of Palestinian Americans to ever be elected at the state level across the US.&nbsp;

Kawas posted a short&nbsp;message to X after the victory saying, "We made history".&nbsp;

"Fund dignity, fight destruction" was the slogan Kawas campaigned with. "Fund dignity" refers to her stance on healthcare for all, fast and reliable public transportation, and affordable housing. "Fight destruction" refers to her positions on stopping climate change, protecting immigrants and her "No money for war" stance.

Kawas is a member of the Democratic Socialists of America, a group that has made unprecedented gains since its member Zohran Mamdani won the historic mayoral race in New York City last year.&nbsp;









She was among the growing slate of Democratic Socialists of America candidates that ran in this cycle.

Many progressive Democrats endorsed Kawas, including Mamdani, Senator Bernie Sanders, and Congresswoman Rashida Tlaib.

Her win reflects the growing footprint of the pro-Palestine community within the US political landscape.&nbsp;

'Not On Our Dime'

Before entering politics, Kawas spent more than 15 years as an organiser fighting for workers' rights, immigrant communities, and Palestinian rights.&nbsp;

Last month, Kawas told Middle East Eye that in 2023 she was part of a coalition of activists looking to work at a local level of government to disrupt funding that supports the Israeli state and its military occupation of Palestine.




