They discovered several charities in the US that had raised millions of dollars to send Jewish Americans to the occupied West Bank as settlers on Palestinian land in violation of international law.&nbsp;

Kawas helped develop and launch the "Not On Our Dime" campaign, which seeks to prohibit New York nonprofits from funding organisations that support Israel's illegal occupation of the Palestinian territories.

"It shouldn't be allowed in New York state law for a group that is registered as a charity organisation - which should be collecting money to help people - to take that money and commit human rights atrocities," Kawas told MEE.&nbsp;

To introduce the bill, she worked with Mamdani, then a New York City Assembly member, and State Senator Jabari Brisport, who sponsored and authored the bill in May 2023.&nbsp;

But due to strong opposition from lawmakers, the legislation titled "Ending New York funding of Israeli settler violence act" never became a law.&nbsp;

"It was interesting to work with then-Assembly member Mamdani because I was sceptical of politicians," Kawas told MEE, explaining that this process and bill inspired her.

"It was interesting to work with him as an elected official and see how he could use that position to do something that was unprecedented.

"When we had introduced the Not On Our Dime bill, that had never been done before in New York State; nobody had ever proactively introduced something that was fighting for Palestinian human rights," she said.









End collaboration with ICE

Kawas was born and raised in Brooklyn to Palestinian parents who immigrated to the US in the 1990s. She said despite her parents being undocumented at the time, she felt accepted in her community. But that sense of safety shifted when Kawas was in the fourth grade, following the aftermath of the 9/11 attacks.

She described how fear in the New York Muslim community of being surveilled, deported or facing unfounded terror charges was pervasive. Even in elementary school, her classmates began to distance themselves from her.&nbsp;

As a child of immigrants, Kawas takes a pro-immigration stance and vows to end New York's collaboration with Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) by passing the New York For All Act, which would prohibit the state and local agencies from cooperating with federal civil immigration enforcement.

"I think it's really important to talk about the idea that it destroys entire lives and families. It's not just the one person - oftentimes you will see a story, and the story is two people got picked up by ICE, or one person gets picked up by ICE off the street, and that one person has an entire family around them," she said.

Kawas knows all too well the effects of having a family member deported or detained. Her father was deported to Jordan when she was in high school. Kawas explained to MEE how her dad's detention and ultimate deportation affected her family.&nbsp;

"Having someone taken out of a family unit, in many ways, shifts everything. It shifts our lives economically. We weren't able to have as much money, afford the same things," she said.&nbsp;

"Every one of my siblings had to work from a really young age to be able to help my mom afford to take care of us. That's an economic shift and a psychological shift."&nbsp;









Palestinian activism&nbsp;

Kawas is challenging the status quo of "Progressive except for Palestine" politics. Before the Hamas-led attacks on southern Israel on 7 October 2023 and the subsequent genocide in Gaza, when she spoke to elected Democrats about Palestine-related human rights issues, she got little response.&nbsp;

"People believed that to have a political career, you couldn't speak for Palestinian human rights, that you could never speak out against the state of Israel; that would be political suicide," she told MEE.&nbsp;

While campaigning, she has not shied away from her Palestinian identity. Earlier this year, she was arrested for protesting against Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's arrival in New York City at the United Nations General Assembly, where she was among dozens arrested, and later released.&nbsp;




