"It felt really meaningful to participate in civil disobedience with other elected officials and politicians," Kawas said.&nbsp;

"I grew up in a time period where you just never had an elected official stand up for Palestinian human rights. So, to be amongst the ten or more politicians, at that moment I thought it was really cool. I wish we could have done more. I really wish that Netanyahu never entered the city."&nbsp;

Earlier this month, she voiced opposition to a $750,000 deal between the New York Police Department (NYPD) and Israeli surveillance company Cognyte to develop AI-powered mobile phone tracking.

"This powerful tool violates the rights of all New Yorkers, giving the police greater ability to track our phones and access sensitive data with little transparency or oversight," Kawas wrote on X.

"However, this is about more than just one company or contract. The NYPD has a long history of mass surveillance of Muslim communities, Palestine solidarity activists, and communities of colour, and police departments across the state are sharing AI surveillance data with ICE to aid in the deportation of our immigrant neighbours."&nbsp;

Last week, the NYPD backed out of the deal, following backlash from civil rights groups.

As a Palestinian who is unabashedly vocal on Palestine, Kawas is the embodiment of shifting political tides.&nbsp;

"New Yorkers are asking for a change in the administration. We are actually really hopeful that we have a system of community safety that is actually interested in people's lives and not interested in continuing these patterns of policing that are violent and that are affecting so many New Yorkers," she said.

