    Read More »


The irony is that two crises aimed at inflicting a strategic defeat, and ideally regime change, in both Russia and Iran, have blown up in the faces of western liberal democracies, which are facing unbearable and dangerous costs.&nbsp;

While Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent has unsuccessfully tried to reduce US bond yields, further fuelling investors’ concerns, Trump has been scrambling to find a way to end the conflict in Ukraine, aiming to better focus on his war against Iran after the midterms. It is not clear yet whether Russia, Iran, and above all China will allow him such room for maneuver.&nbsp;

Several crucial issues must now be monitored closely for developments, including the outcome of Israel’s elections, which resulted in a deadlock last week; will the incoming government continue to bamboozle the US into waging an unnecessary war against Iran?

Ongoing European economic negotiations will also be vital to watch, alongside the outcome of Tuesday’s US midterm vote and the impacts on markets and investments. In addition, what happens next in relation to the chokepoints at Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb will be crucial: if commodity flows normalise, prices could drop within months, and the storm may lose its force.&nbsp;

If not, winter will be the next stress test - and it is fast approaching.

